e they're censored there DW has a  huge duty to provide freedom of  information and then there's the entire  set of values that we also have to  impart the liberal constitutional  democracy that DW represents in which it  propagates is crumbling in Poland  Hungary Russia Turkey  how does DW reacts these developments we  try to refer especially to the issues in  those places where we have a broadcast  presence in Turkey in Russia and in  Poland we try to inform our users  listeners or viewers and give them the  outside perspective we can't tell anyone  what they should think that's not our  duty and we always have to ensure that  we manage to remain available to a wide  target audience but we can guarantee  freedom of information and also point  out that democratic rights aren't a  luxury or negotiable in the EU for  example when there's a growing number of  people who don't consider democratic  rights to be that important who wants  security does that mean DW the voice of  democracy is talking at cross-purposes  for more and more people no I don't  think so I think for us also in  countries where democracy is in danger  and there is a real lack of freedom is  to be there for those who do believe in  democracy  history has shown us that whether in  Poland or Greece during times of a  dictatorship the DW played an  outstanding role as the voice of freedom  perhaps we are only accessible there for  a limited time and only for a minority  simply because not everyone can access  our services  but we want to offer that minority  support so that people in countries  where democracy is at risk you can see  there are people on the outside who care  about them we're not really there yet in  Poland and other parts of Europe it's  important to us that we don't point a  warning finger at them but continue to  demonstrate the merits of democracy  before to the Democrats you know Vida  want to watch their right-wing populism  is on the rise in Germany as well the  AFD party is entering parliaments what  can journalists do to bring these angry  citizens back to the fold  I don't believe that it's d-double news  job to confront the Germans in Germany  with topic matter in any way our main  task is clearly to address overseas  audiences but we do believe it makes  sense for our programming in English  Arabic and Spanish to be made available  in Germany as well in order to build a  bridge between different nations and  languages in Sweden  I think that the unrest in Germany and  the rise of right-wing populism is  something that's happening across Europe  we're no exception it is nevertheless  dangerous when these political parties  try to chip away at democracy when they  don't just express themselves in a  so-called nationalist manner but when  they stray from the basic principles of  democracy it becomes dangerous and the  job of the media is to identify that and  to make every instance of it public and  not to capitulate or stay silent or  accept the fact that someone is  attacking democracy democracy otherwise  that's good and that also applies to the  US where right-wing populism is in  charge  DW awarded a prize for defending Press  Freedom to the White House  Correspondents Association in Washington  do you believe press freedom is in more  danger in the US  than in Russia or China for example  non-decision no certainly not the u.s.  is a great democracy which has shown  that it can overcome many challenges  naturally we hope that it will overcome  the current ones but it seldom occurs  that the head of state in an established  democracy questions democratic rights  and that includes freedom of the press  which is a prerequisite for democracy  and that is extraordinary and that's  when we say we have to highlight events  there as well and show that we don't  just focus all our attention on the  southern hemisphere Russia whether we  focus on everywhere where press freedom  is threatened in the u.s. that is partly  the case right now Dona